1. Definitions

"You" means the individual or Organization exercising rights under these
terms.

"Licensor" means the copyright owner or an entity authorized by the copyright
owner to offer the relevant part of the Software under these terms.

"JooEvents project steward" means the original copyright owner publishing as
Bowlboy, or a successor publicly identified as steward by the canonical
JooEvents repository.

"Organization" means any legal entity, sole proprietorship, unincorporated
association, public body, or other organization, together with every entity
that controls, is controlled by, or is under common control with it. "Control"
means the direct or indirect power to direct management or policies, or
ownership of fifty percent or more of the voting or beneficial interests.

"Worker" means an employee or an independent contractor who regularly works
for an Organization. Part-time Workers count as individuals, not fractions.

"Annual Resources" means the greater of an Organization's consolidated gross
revenue and its consolidated annual operating budget for its most recently
completed fiscal year. If the Organization has not completed a fiscal year,
use a reasonable good-faith estimate for its first year.

"Small Organization" means an Organization that has both:

  (a) fewer than 50 Workers; and
  (b) less than US$5,000,000 in Annual Resources.

"Production Use" means use of the Software for an Organization's operations,
or to provide a live service, as opposed to evaluation, development, testing,
security research, or demonstration.

"Commercial Offering" means any product or service in which You, directly or
indirectly for payment or commercial advantage:

  (a) sell, license, rent, lease, or otherwise provide the Software or a
      modified version of it;
  (b) provide third parties with hosted or managed access to a substantial
      portion of the Software's functionality;
  (c) embed a substantial portion of the Software in a product or service
      offered to third parties; or
  (d) white-label or resell the Software or its functionality.

Charging for consulting, installation, migration, customization, training, or
support is not by itself a Commercial Offering when the customer separately
has the right to use the Software and the deployment is dedicated to and
controlled by that customer.

Using the Software to organize an Organization's own events—including access
for its applicants, speakers, reviewers, attendees, sponsors, volunteers, and
staff—is not by itself a Commercial Offering, even when the Organization
charges admission, sponsorship, participation, or submission fees. Operating
events for customers as a hosted, white-labelled, or managed JooEvents service
may still be a Commercial Offering.

"Source Form" means the preferred form for making modifications. "Object
Form" means any form produced by mechanical transformation or translation of
Source Form.

"Modified Work" means any work based on or derived from the Software. It does
not include a work that merely communicates with the Software through its
documented interfaces and is otherwise separable from it.

2. Rights available to everyone

Subject to these terms, the Licensor grants You a worldwide, non-exclusive,
royalty-free copyright license to:

  (a) inspect, evaluate, develop, test, study, and demonstrate the Software;
  (b) reproduce and modify the Software for those purposes; and
  (c) distribute the Software or a Modified Work, in Source or Object Form,
      without charge and not as part of a Commercial Offering.

This means that public, noncommercial forks are welcome. Donations or
sponsorships that are not required to receive or use a copy do not, by
themselves, make a distribution commercial.

3. Free Production Use

Subject to these terms, the Licensor also grants You a worldwide,
non-exclusive, royalty-free copyright license to make Production Use of the
Software when either:

  (a) You are an individual acting on Your own behalf, not for the benefit of
      an Organization or as a business, for personal, educational, research,
      volunteer, or community purposes; or
  (b) the Organization benefiting from the Production Use is a Small
      Organization.

This Section does not permit a Commercial Offering. A separate commercial
license is required for a Commercial Offering regardless of the size of the
person or Organization providing it.

4. Consultants and service providers

You may charge for professional services relating to the Software without
obtaining a reseller license when:

  (a) the customer has rights to make Production Use under Section 3,
      Section 6, or a separate commercial license;
  (b) each deployment is dedicated to and controlled by that customer; and
  (c) You do not sublicense the Software, provide shared hosted access, or
      present Yourself as an official JooEvents provider.

5. Conditions on distribution and forks

Every distribution permitted by Section 2 must:

  (a) include a complete copy of these terms;
  (b) retain applicable copyright, patent, attribution, and legal notices;
  (c) identify files You materially changed; and
  (d) license the Software and every Modified Work, as a whole, under these
      same terms.

Recipients receive their rights directly from the Licensor under these terms.
You may not impose terms that restrict or conflict with the rights granted by
these terms. Separate works that merely communicate with the Software through
documented interfaces are not subject to this Section.

6. Larger-organization courtesy grant and commercial offerings

Subject to these terms, an Organization that is not a Small Organization may
make Production Use of the Software for its own operations and events without
first obtaining a separate commercial license. This is a worldwide,
non-exclusive, royalty-free copyright license that continues unless and until
the Licensor sends that Organization a written Commercial License Notice.

A Commercial License Notice is valid only if it:

  (a) is sent from bowlboy@joocorp.com to an email address the Organization
      has provided or publicly identifies for legal, licensing, or general
      business inquiries;
  (b) identifies the Organization and this Section; and
  (c) asks the Organization to obtain a separate commercial license or stop
      Production Use.

After receiving a valid Commercial License Notice, the Organization may
continue its existing Production Use for 180 days while the parties discuss a
commercial license or the Organization prepares to stop that use. No fee,
royalty, damages, or other payment is owed for Production Use authorized before
or during that 180-day period. If the parties do not agree on a separate
license, the courtesy grant terminates when the period ends and the Organization
must stop Production Use, but may retain copies and continue the non-production
activities permitted by Section 2. Stopping by the deadline fully satisfies the
notice; no retroactive license fee is due.

The courtesy grant in this Section does not permit a Commercial Offering.
Every Commercial Offering requires a separate written commercial license from
the Licensor or an authorized licensing representative before it begins.
Commercial licenses may use a fixed fee, a per-customer or per-deployment fee,
royalties, or another mutually agreed structure. Pricing is not publicly
disclosed. Commercial-license inquiries may be sent to bowlboy@joocorp.com.

These terms do not obligate the Licensor to offer a commercial license or any
particular commercial terms.

